\section*{Worst-case symmetric translative covering density}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 092; 2026-09-23), the asserted result is that in every sufficiently large dimension, some symmetric convex body has translative covering density at least an absolute multiple of $n\log n$, ruling out a uniform linear bound.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
For a convex body $K\subset\mathbb R^n$, define its translative covering density by
\[
 \theta_T(K)=\inf_{\substack{X\subset\mathbb R^n\text{ locally finite}\\K+X=\mathbb R^n}}
 |K|\limsup_{R\to\infty}\frac{|X\cap[-R,R]^n|}{(2R)^n}.
\]
The covering centers are not required to form a lattice. The claimed lower bound is
\[
 \exists c>0\ \exists n_0\ \forall n\ge n_0\ \exists K_n=-K_n:
 \quad\theta_T(K_n)>c n\log n,
\]
with $c,n_0$ absolute and every $K_n$ a full-dimensional convex body.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 092; 2026-09-23), a random body is tested against a finite family obtained by rounding periodic covering patterns. Cap-intersection and Poisson-witness estimates make successful low-density coverage too unlikely for any pattern; periodization extends the obstruction to all coverings.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The result supplies the conclusion in this problem with its displayed hypotheses and quantifiers. The broader statements and applications in the manuscript are kept separate from the particular question above. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 092; 2026-09-23).

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 092; 2026-09-23), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: in every sufficiently large dimension, some symmetric convex body has translative covering density at least an absolute multiple of $n\log n$, ruling out a uniform linear bound.

The central assertion is within the documented formal scope; the additional applications retain their own scope limits.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{CoveringDensity.lean}, \texttt{SingleLatticeCovering.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.CoveringOrder.optimal\_order}, \texttt{OAI.SingleLatticeCovering.single\_lattice\_covering}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

